	'ip_about_close' => 'Marguerite still walks the grass every morning before the gates open. The people who run the stalls are introduced on the stallholders page.',
	'ip_about_h1' => 'About Hozue Food Festival',
	'ip_about_lede' => 'Hozue Food Festival began in 2016 as a small gathering of local producers along the riverbank in Alderford. Now preparing for our tenth edition in September 2027, the weekend remains committed to thoughtful regional food, independent winemakers, and sustainable stewardship of the riverside park.',
	'ip_about_s1_b' => 'Marguerite Ostby founded Hozue Food Festival in 2016 with a modest row of trestle tables along the water. Her intention was straightforward: to connect regional growers and independent producers directly with neighbours who care about honest cooking. Over the past decade, that small gathering has grown into our annual September weekend in Hozue Riverside Park. Marguerite still directs the festival, walking the grass each morning before the gates open on Weir Lane. While the gathering has welcomed more producers, its scale remains deliberately grounded, keeping the focus on conversations across the counter, seasonal ingredients, and the quiet rhythm of the river.',
	'ip_about_s1_h' => 'Ten Years Along the Riverbank',
	'ip_about_s2_b' => 'Every stallholder at Hozue is invited based on a simple standard: local roots, seasonal focus, and own produce. We bring together sixty-four stalls and eighteen winemakers who work their own land, bake their own loaves, or tend their own herds. You will meet market gardeners bringing morning harvests, cheesemakers presenting washed-rind wheels from small dairies, and vintners who pour their own bottles. We choose makers who prioritise care over volume, ensuring that every plate and glass shared across the park reflects genuine regional craft. Stallholders manage their own menus, setting honest prices and taking the time to share their methods with visitors.',
	'ip_about_s2_h' => 'How We Choose Our Stalls',
	'ip_about_s3_b' => 'As Sunday evening arrives and the final pours finish at the river terrace, the quiet work of packing down begins. Story three of our festival life belongs to the clear-up team and the compost tent. From the start, Marguerite established a zero-waste target for food packaging across the grounds. Every plate, fork, and napkin used during the weekend must be compostable, and tasting glasses are collected for return or kept for future years. By midnight on Sunday, the compost tent has sorted organic waste from all sixty-four stalls, leaving Hozue Riverside Park clean, restored, and open for morning walkers.',
